{"packet_id":"b909a269ba3848b7","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies a consumer proposition) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives: product-line extension, no expansion, and route-to-market change without product change. It sets a value-capture constraint and flags consumer reach as unknown. It does not reach rung 3. It never examines what the 10-Q 'Sales direct to consumer' line means (A4/A5 retrieved it). It does not identify a specific use case that the dossier points to, such as the wet-dry hard-floor segment in tennant-tineco-wetdry."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The best theory, T2 (A10), names a benefit: time and cost savings lead to premium purchases. It does not explain why households would switch from existing consumer wet-dry machines to Tennant, or how Tennant's professional assets (the field service network in tennant-ir-overview) would capture value. 'Repeat purchases' and 'additional units' of a durable machine are asserted without a mechanism. T1 (A9), status-driven multi-unit buying 'for display', is weaker still."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (status/lifestyle) and T2 (functional advantage) are rival explanations of consumer adoption. Both assume the same customers, the same retail channel and essentially the same product, differing mainly in messaging. They do not imply different products, customers or channels."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"Assumptions are stated, but the predictions are vague or near-tautological. Examples: 'demand for a differentiated proposition would be higher than a purely functional proposition' if a survey majority says they would buy (A9), and 'sales of additional units would be higher than for a single unit' (A9). T2's prediction (A10) presumes consumers 'currently using standard scrubbers'. None specifies an outcome whose failure would clearly put the theory in trouble."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A11","A12","A2","A3","A6","A8"],"reason":"Only two assessments were made. A12 counts a company marketing claim about the T360, a professional mid-tier walk-behind scrubber 'helpful to onboarding first-time users', as supporting a consumer functional-advantage theory. It does so while admitting the claim gives no evidence of time or cost savings. A11 treats the 10-Q channel line as neutral without asking whether 'direct to consumer' denotes households. The run retrieved but never assessed highly relevant verified observations: Kärcher's roughly equal private/professional split, Nilfisk consumer growth of 19.7% on under 10% of sales, Tineco's 24 million users and retail presence in the wet-dry segment, and Kärcher 2025 market headwinds (A2, A3, A6, A8)."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13"],"reason":"A13 runs a single challenge search against T1 and retrieves tennant-nilfisk-consumer-share, tennant-ir-overview and tennant-tineco-wetdry. It never reports what they show or whether they bear on T1. No challenge to T2 was attempted. T1 is later rated 'likely' (A15) despite no supporting evidence."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A14"],"reason":"The decision rule contradicts the stated predictions. T1 predicts a score below 5, yet a score below 5 'concludes T1 is not supported'. T2 predicts a score above 7, yet a score above 7 'concludes T2 is not supported'. Both outcomes lead to 'no proposition', so the test cannot separate the theories as specified. It measures stated intent, not purchase behaviour. Its stimulus is the T360 professional scrubber. The €20,000 cost and n=500 sample are assumptions consuming the entire budget, with no observed results."},"revision":{"status":"not_applicable","score":null,"event_ids":[],"reason":"Revision is excluded from packet.applicable_dimensions. scheduled_update is false and update_delivered is false."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A15","A16","A14"],"reason":"It takes a position (do not invest yet; run X1) and names a next action. However, it favours T1 as 'likely' with no source supporting T1. Its proceed condition, 'low performance-importance score' leads to building the proposition, contradicts the A14 decision rule, where a score below 5 leads to no proposition. The recommendation and uncertainty text are truncated mid-sentence ('If the', 'justify a differentiated'). It does not follow from the evidence."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A12"],"reason":"A company marketing claim about a professional mid-tier scrubber is counted as support for a consumer functional-advantage theory, despite the model admitting it gives no evidence of savings."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A15","A16","A9"],"reason":"T1 (status-driven multi-unit consumer purchases) is rated 'likely' and becomes the favoured basis for the value proposition. No assessed source supports it; the only T1 assessment (A11) is neutral."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A14"],"reason":"The decision rule inverts the predictions: each theory's predicted outcome is read as refuting it, and both extremes lead to the same 'no proposition' decision, so the test cannot separate T1 from T2."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A14","A16"],"reason":"The recommendation's proceed condition contradicts the test's decision rule."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A15","A16"],"reason":"Position, recommendation and uncertainty text are truncated mid-sentence."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A2","A3","A6","A8"],"reason":"Highly relevant verified competitor evidence (Kärcher, Nilfisk, Tineco) was retrieved but never assessed."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"Rejected. The run favours T1 with no supporting evidence, treats a professional-product marketing claim as consumer evidence, and proposes a test whose decision rule inverts its own predictions and cannot discriminate between the theories. The final recommendation contradicts the test rule and is truncated. Each of these is a critical error."}
